<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aachen Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 796–805</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> Cathedral, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, has as its core the octagonal Palatine Chapel built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> around 796–805, where he was buried in 814. From 936 to 1531 it was the coronation church of German kings, and a Gothic choir was added in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. It was the first German site on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List, in 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral.jpg" alt="Aachen Cathedral" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aachen_Germany_Imperial-Cathedral-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> began the Palatine Chapel around 796 with the rest of the palace buildings, and the work is credited to Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>. A letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> in 798 says it was nearing completion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> consecrated it in 805. The octagon followed Byzantine models such as the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna and the Little Hagia Sophia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>The design was a domed octagonal inner room, 14.46 metres across, enveloped by a sixteen-sided outer wall, with a high gallery called the Hochmünster. The high altar and imperial throne stood on the upper level. Its span and height were unsurpassed north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> for over two hundred years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> died on 28 January 814 and was buried in the chapel, though the exact spot is unknown and he was probably laid in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a> sarcophagus. Otto III had the vault opened in the year 1000, and the Chronicle of Novalesia, written about 1026, reports that the emperor was found seated and crowned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> reopened it in 1165 at Charlemagne's canonisation, and in 1215 Frederick II placed the remains in the gold and silver Karlsschrein, where they remain.</p><p>Between 936 and 1531 thirty-one German kings ascended the throne in the western gallery after their coronation at the altar of Mary. The Carolingian throne used material from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s canonisation, an enormous flow of pilgrims led to the Gothic choir hall of 1355, and the relics in the Marienschrein of 1220–1239 have been shown every seven years since 1349.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The chapel was admired and imitated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, in Essen Minster, the Old Tower at Mettlach and the abbey church at Ottmarsheim. It suffered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raid in 881 and was restored in 983, lost its entire roof in the Fire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> of 1656, and had its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> stucco removed in 1881. In the Second World War Allied bombing and ground fighting heavily damaged the city; the basic structure of the cathedral survived, but the 14th-century choir glazing, the Gothic Revival altar, much of the cloister and the Holiness Chapel were destroyed. Reconstruction took more than 30 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aachen Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The palace chapel at Aachen survives as the core of <b>Aachen Cathedral</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>King Otto I, who had named his six-year-old son Otto II heir apparent and co-ruler at Worms, had him crowned in <b>Aachen Cathedral</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-26">26 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard of Cornwall, the second son of King John of England, and his wife Sanchia of Provence were crowned King and Queen of the Romans in <b>Aachen Cathedral</b>, the coronation church of German kings from 936 to 1531.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-27">27 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rudolf of Habsburg, a Swabian count elected King of the Romans that year, was crowned in <b>Aachen Cathedral</b>, the first of his house to hold the title.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-24">24 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aachen Cathedral</b> was among the first World Heritage Sites.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Free Imperial City of Aachen</a></li></ul></section>
